12:06:44 said  The rebuilt app is a native desktop embroidery workspace that opens straight into the design table with no USB key, no licence screen and no sign-in — and it contains no networking code at all (I checked while it was running: zero internet connections). You can draw filled, satin and run stitches, pick hoops and thread colours, reorder and edit objects, play a stitch-by-stitch simulation with stitch counts and sewing time, and save designs or export DST, SVG and CSV; I also verified that moving, undoing and saving actually work by clicking through the app.
12:06:44 said  What differs: there were no pictures or description of the original screen, so the dark styling, panel layout and the sample flower are my interpretation of a Janome-style digitizing workspace, not a copy of the original. The exported stitch file has been decoded back and checked against the design, but it has not been sewn on a real machine. Opening Janome's own design files is not supported yet — only the universal DST stitch format can be imported.
12:06:44 said  Next, the most useful thing would be to send me one of your existing design files so I can add direct support for opening it, or tell me which two or three tools you use most so I can put them first.
